      teachingContent:
        '### The Oil That Fuels the Flame\n\nThe golden candlestick, for all its beauty, was utterly useless without one essential element: **the pure olive oil** that fed its seven lamps. Exodus 27:20 describes it as "pure oil olive beaten for the light, to cause the lamp to burn always." The oil did not originate within the candlestick itself; it was supplied from without, pressed from olives that grew in the groves of Israel and carefully refined until only the purest liquid remained. This is a profound sanctuary lesson for every witness: we do not generate our own spiritual light. The power to shine comes entirely from the Holy Spirit, the divine Oil who fills and fuels the believer. Without Him, we are ornamental gold — impressive to look at, perhaps, but producing no illumination whatsoever. Every effective witness in the history of redemption has been a Spirit-filled vessel, not a self-sufficient performer.\n\n### Zechariah\'s Vision of Continuous Supply\n\nThe prophet Zechariah was given a remarkable vision that expands our understanding of this truth. He saw a golden candlestick with a bowl upon the top of it, and two olive trees standing on either side, pouring oil directly and continuously into the bowl through golden pipes (Zechariah 4:2-3). When Zechariah asked what this meant, the angel answered with words that should be engraved on the heart of every Christian witness: "Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit, saith the LORD of hosts" (Zechariah 4:6). The vision revealed a candlestick that never ran dry because it was **directly connected to the source**. The olive trees fed the bowl, the bowl fed the lamps, and the light never went out. This is God\'s design for us — not a reservoir that we fill up on Sabbath and hope will last until Friday, but a living, continuous connection to the Holy Spirit who supplies fresh oil moment by moment.\n\n### Pentecost and Power for Witness\n\nThe fulfillment of this sanctuary type burst forth on the day of Pentecost. "And when the day of Pentecost was fully come, they were all with one accord in one place. And suddenly there came a sound from heaven as of a rushing mighty wind, and it filled all the house where they were sitting. And there appeared unto them cloven tongues like as of fire, and it sat upon each of them. And they were all filled with the Holy Ghost" (Acts 2:1-4). Notice the imagery — **tongues of fire** resting upon each believer, just as flames burned upon each branch of the candlestick. The disciples did not go out to witness in their own eloquence or cleverness; they went out ablaze with the Spirit\'s power, and three thousand souls were converted in a single day. Jesus had promised, "When he, the Spirit of truth, is come, he will guide you into all truth" and "he will reprove the world of sin, and of righteousness, and of judgment" (John 16:8, 13). The Spirit not only empowers our words but also works upon the hearts of those who hear, convicting them of their need for a Saviour.\n\n### Receiving the Oil Today\n\nHow do we receive this oil? The same way the early church did — through earnest, united prayer, through surrender of self-will, through obedience to the light already given, and through a genuine hunger for God\'s presence. Ellen White wrote that the Holy Spirit is waiting to be sought, waiting to be asked, waiting to be received. He will not force Himself upon us, but He will abundantly fill every vessel that is emptied of self and presented to God. If your witness has felt dry, forced, or fruitless, the issue is almost certainly an oil supply problem. The candlestick is not broken; it simply needs to be reconnected to the source. This week, before we discuss any method or strategy, we must settle the question of power. Kneel before God and ask Him to fill you afresh with the pure oil of the Holy Spirit, and He will.',
